[s0l0!-header][h1]The Object Constructor[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

In the previous lesson, we created an object using the [b]object literal[/b] (or initializer) syntax.[code format="js" ]var person = {
name: "John", age: 42, favColor: "green"
};[/code]
This allows you to create only a single object.
Sometimes, we need to set an "[b]object type[/b]" that can be used to create a number of objects of a single type.
The standard way to create an "object type" is to use an object [b]constructor function[/b]. [code format="js" ]function person(name, age, color) {
  this.name = name;
  this.age = age;
  this.favColor = color;
}[/code]
The above function (person) is an object constructor, which takes parameters and assigns them to the object properties. 
[note]The [b]this [/b]keyword refers to the [b]current object[/b].
Note that [b]this [/b]is not a variable. It is a keyword, and its value cannot be changed.[/note]

